<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=telescope">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Telescope</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1608">1608</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A telescope is an instrument for observing distant objects by the radiation they emit, absorb or reflect; the first known practical telescopes, refractors with glass lenses, were invented in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> at the beginning of the 17th century. The earliest record of one is a patent application of 1608 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middelburg">Middelburg</a> spectacle maker Hans Lipperhey, though the actual inventor is unknown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a> built his own version in 1609 and used it to observe celestial objects, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a> built the first practical reflecting telescope in 1668.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/telescope.jpg" alt="Telescope" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rama · CC BY-SA 2.0 fr · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lunette_mg_2368.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word telescope was coined in 1611 by the Greek mathematician Giovanni Demisiani for one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a>'s instruments, presented at a banquet of the Accademia dei Lincei. In the Starry Messenger Galileo had used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> term perspicillum. The Greek roots are tele, far, and skopein, to look or see, giving teleskopos, far-seeing. The first practical instruments were refracting telescopes with glass lenses, used both on land and for astronomy. Word of the invention spread through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and Galileo heard of it before building his own version.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Originally the telescope was an optical instrument that used lenses, curved mirrors or both to observe distant objects. The word now covers a wide range of instruments that detect different regions of the electromagnetic spectrum. There are three main optical types: the refracting telescope, which forms an image with lenses; the reflecting telescope, which uses mirrors; and the catadioptric telescope, which combines mirrors with lenses. A place holding one or more telescopes or other instruments is called an observatory.</p><p>Radio telescopes are directional antennas that usually employ a large dish. By correlating the signals received by several dishes, astronomers compute high-resolution images, a technique called aperture synthesis. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/x-ray">X-rays</a> are harder to focus, and X-ray telescopes use ring-shaped mirrors of heavy metals that reflect the rays at a glancing angle; Hans Wolter outlined three ways of building such a telescope in 1952. Because the atmosphere is opaque to most of the spectrum, ultraviolet, X-ray and gamma-ray instruments are carried on balloons or satellites.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The idea that the light-gathering element could be a mirror instead of a lens was investigated soon after the refracting telescope appeared. Parabolic mirrors promised less spherical aberration and no chromatic aberration. In 1668 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a> built the first practical reflecting telescope, of the design now called the Newtonian reflector. The achromatic lens of 1733 partly corrected the colour fringes of simple lenses and allowed shorter, more useful refractors.</p><p>Reflectors were long hampered by mirrors of speculum metal, which tarnished quickly in the 18th and early 19th centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a>-coated glass mirrors, introduced in 1857, and aluminized mirrors, from 1932, eased the problem. Refractors cannot grow beyond about one metre across, so most large research telescopes built since the turn of the 20th century are reflectors. John Dobson invented the Dobsonian telescope in 1956. The first purpose-built radio telescope began operating in 1937, and infrared telescopes followed in the 1960s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Telescope” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ulugh Beg's Samarkand star catalogue of over a thousand stars was among the most accurate before the <b>telescope</b>, and was published at Oxford in 1665.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>From Uraniborg, his observatory on the island of Hven, he made the most accurate observations recorded before the <b>telescope</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Galileo showed the Venetian authorities his improved <b>telescope</b>, which made distant ships visible hours before they could be seen by eye.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-25">25 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Through his improved <b>telescope</b>, Galileo saw small 'stars' beside Jupiter that he soon realised were orbiting it.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-7">7 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It proved to be a new planet, the first discovered with a <b>telescope</b>, doubling the known size of the solar system.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-13">13 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Using a <b>telescope</b> he had built with his brother, Huygens found Saturn's largest moon.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-25">25 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1608</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Middelburg</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Hans Lipperhey · <a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The actual inventor is unknown; Lipperhey’s patent application of 1608 is the earliest record of a telescope.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/eyeglasses">Eyeglasses<span>c. 1290</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/optical-telegraph">Optical telegraph<span>1792</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=telescope">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
